<h3>1. Start every project with <code>/init</code></h3>
<p>Bringing Claude Code into a project that already has code? Don't overthink it — the first command is <code>/init</code>. Claude reads through the codebase and writes a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with build commands, test instructions, and project conventions. That file is a project guide read automatically at the start of every session, so you stop re-explaining "so, this project is…" every time. If a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> already exists it suggests improvements instead of overwriting, so run it without worry.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How Claude remembers your project</a> — this wiki's <a href="#!ccb-overview">Basics-chapter CLAUDE.md hands-on</a> and <a href="#!claude-md-commandments">CLAUDE.md commandments</a> cover the same ground.</p>

<h3>2. Turn on the status line</h3>
<p>Working in the terminal? Turn on the status bar with <code>/statusline</code>. The model in use, the git branch, and — the big one — <strong>the remaining context percentage</strong> stay visible under the input box. Once you can see the gauge, habits 3 through 5 below stick on their own. The desktop app already shows this, so nothing to set up there.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/statusline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Status line configuration</a> — the displayed items are fully scriptable.</p>

<h3>3. Keep the context small</h3>
<p>A command like "review the entire codebase" burns tens of percent of your context in one shot. For big problems, <strong>start with the splitting</strong> — not "fix the payment system" but "break the payment system fix into small steps," then run each step separately. However smart the model is, once context runs dry it forgets what you said earlier.</p>

<h3>4. Find the token hogs with <code>/context</code></h3>
<p>Context draining weirdly fast? Type <code>/context</code>. It shows you at a glance which files, tools, and messages are taking how much. The culprit is usually one of three: a large file read whole, an MCP server that loaded its full tool catalog (tip 22 in the advanced part), or a conversation history that grew and grew.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/commands" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Slash commands reference</a></p>

<h3>5. Around 60%, run <code>/compact</code>; between tasks, run <code>/clear</code></h3>
<p>Don't mix these two up. <code>/compact</code> <strong>summarizes and compresses</strong> the history — you can say what to keep ("keep the decisions"), and the thread continues. <code>/clear</code> is a <strong>full reset</strong>, same as opening a new conversation. When one task ends and another begins, reset rather than compress — leftovers from the old task can't contaminate the new one.</p>

