<h3>6. Start in plan mode</h3>
<p>Plan mode touches no files — it only <strong>reads, analyzes, and plans</strong> (switch with <code>Shift+Tab</code> in the terminal). Since nothing changes, you can hand over anything with peace of mind, and while planning Claude asks you questions that narrow your intent. The lazier you feel about writing a detailed prompt, the more plan mode earns its keep — it asks the questions for you.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">One thing worth knowing — since 14 August 2026, new sessions default to <strong>auto mode</strong>, which no longer asks approval at every step (<a href="#!news-20260814-auto-mode-stateless-mcp">Defaults That Stop Asking</a>). That makes "plan before touching anything" more important, not less — execution got faster, so agree on the direction first. Mode details: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Permissions and modes</a></p>

<h3>7. Treat it like a junior developer</h3>
<p>The moment you say "write a function that adjusts padding by screen width," you've already done the hardest part yourself — defining the problem and picking the approach. That's using maybe 10% of the AI. Hand over <strong>the problem</strong> instead. Claude can read the whole project and propose an approach, so brainstorm together and let it execute its own plan — that's the flow that uses the tool at full size.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Not like this (you already picked the solution)</span>
Write a function that adjusts padding dynamically by screen width

<span class="c"># Like this (hand over the problem, design the fix together)</span>
The video gets cut off on some devices. What do you think is causing it,
and how should we fix it? Compare two or three approaches for me.</code></pre>

<h3>8. Make Claude ask you back</h3>
<p>When your requirement is fuzzy, tack this one line onto the prompt — <strong>"keep asking me questions until you understand what I want at 95%."</strong> Claude fires off clarifying questions, and once that exchange ends it heads in the right direction from the very start. Answering five questions is a lot cheaper than redoing vague instructions three times.</p>
<pre><code>I want motion graphics with a completely different feel from what we have.
Keep asking me questions until you understand what I want at 95%.
Don't start working until the questions are done.</code></pre>

<h3>9. Plant self-checks into the to-dos</h3>
<p>When you hand over a task list, spell out the checks too. Without them, ten to-dos each finish at 60% completeness, the gaps pile up, and later you burn tokens hunting causes you can't find anymore. Plant the checks and the first deliverable comes out at a visibly different level.</p>
<pre><code>Build the landing page. But follow these rules:
1. After each to-do, open Chrome DevTools and check console errors
2. Do not move to the next to-do until all errors are gone
3. Do not mark an item done until you are 95%+ confident it is complete</code></pre>

